---
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import SEO from "../components/SEO.astro";

const questions = [
  {
    id: "state-moral-legitimacy",
    number: "01",
    title: "Is the State morally legitimate?",
    answer: [
      "No. Even the most benevolent State remains coercive because it claims authority over everyone within a territory, whether they consent or not",
      "If individuals were free to join or leave, it would be a voluntary association rather than a State. State rule is not optional; coercion is therefore inherent in the institution",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "state-criminal-organization",
    number: "02",
    title: "Is the State a criminal organization?",
    answer: [
      "Yes, in a normative sense. The State claims powers—compulsory jurisdiction, taxation, and enforcement—that would be considered coercive if exercised by a voluntary association",
      "Legality does not by itself confer moral legitimacy on those acts",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "government-efficiency",
    number: "03",
    title: "Can government ever be efficient?",
    answer: [
      "Government can perform particular tasks effectively and can sometimes improve measured outcomes. But even the best-designed State faces unavoidable limits of information, economic calculation, incentives, and error correction",
      "Those limits can be reduced, but not eliminated, so observed effectiveness does not by itself establish a general case for coercive authority",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "functions-of-government",
    number: "04",
    title: "What functions should government perform?",
    answer: [
      "There is no predefined list. Functions must be examined individually rather than inherited from convention, constitutions, or political tradition",
      "The relevant question is whether coercive necessity can be established for the function itself",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "burden-of-proof-coercion",
    number: "05",
    title: "Who must prove that coercion is necessary?",
    answer: [
      "Whoever seeks to impose it. Liberty is the baseline, and the burden of proof falls on power",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "democracy-legitimizes-coercion",
    number: "06",
    title: "Does democracy legitimize State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "No. Democratic procedures can determine who exercises power and under what rules, but they do not turn compulsory decisions into consent",
      "Majority approval is a method of collective decision-making, not a moral exemption from the need to justify coercion",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "essential-services-state-coercion",
    number: "07",
    title: "Do essential services justify State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "Not by themselves. A service can be essential, valuable, or socially beneficial without making compulsory provision necessary",
      "The value of an end and the justification for coercive means are separate questions",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "public-goods-government-provision",
    number: "08",
    title: "Do public goods require government provision?",
    answer: [
      "No. Non-excludability and non-rivalry describe a structure of the good, not an institutional conclusion",
      "Voluntary provision can still be possible. Samuelson formalized the structure of collective consumption goods in <em>The Pure Theory of Public Expenditure</em>; it does not follow that the State must provide them",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "free-rider-state-coercion",
    number: "09",
    title: "Does the free-rider problem justify State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "No. The fact that someone can benefit without contributing does not show that a function cannot be sustained voluntarily",
      "Free riding becomes relevant when the same incentive problem produces persistent failure across materially different arrangements",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "failure-voluntary-provision",
    number: "10",
    title: "When does voluntary provision actually fail?",
    answer: [
      "When materially different arrangements fail persistently because the same incentive structure prevents the function from being sustained",
      "One failed provider, underprovision, or a temporary absence is not enough",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "schmidtz-public-goods-free-riders",
    number: "11",
    title: "What does David Schmidtz argue about public goods and free riders?",
    answer: [
      "In <em>The Limits of Government</em>, Schmidtz shows that public goods and free riders do not by themselves justify state authority and that some voluntary arrangements can work",
      "Minimal Tolerance adds a stricter requirement: persistent collapse of voluntary provision must be established before coercion can be considered",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "market-failure-government-intervention",
    number: "12",
    title: "Do market failures justify government intervention?",
    answer: [
      "Not by themselves. Natural monopoly, imperfect information, concentration, coordination problems, or underprovision can identify conditions in which markets perform poorly",
      "They do not by themselves show that government intervention is necessary, that coercion is justified, or that available alternatives fail persistently",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "positive-externalities-government-intervention",
    number: "13",
    title: "Do positive externalities justify government intervention?",
    answer: [
      "No. The fact that an activity benefits third parties does not make it a state function",
      "Benefits to others are compatible with voluntary provision and do not establish a need for coercion",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "libertarianism-state-coercion",
    number: "14",
    title: "Can a libertarian theory tolerate State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "Yes, exceptionally. Minimal Tolerance begins from liberty and rejects the moral legitimacy of coercion",
      "It differs from stricter libertarian positions only in allowing coercion to be tolerated when necessity leaves no viable voluntary alternative",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "hayek-government-power",
    number: "15",
    title: "What limits government power in Hayek's political theory?",
    answer: [
      "Hayek emphasizes the limits of knowledge, spontaneous order, general rules, and the rule of law",
      "Minimal Tolerance asks a prior question: which functions may be placed under coercive authority at all",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "buchanan-constitutional-political-economy",
    number: "16",
    title: "What limits government in Buchanan's constitutional political economy?",
    answer: [
      "Buchanan examines the constitutional rules within which collective decisions are made and the role of agreement",
      "Minimal Tolerance moves one step earlier by asking whether a function may be coercively assigned before its institutional design is chosen",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "rothbard-anarcho-capitalism-state-coercion",
    number: "17",
    title: "Does Rothbard's anarcho-capitalism reject all State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "Yes. Rothbard treats coercion as morally illegitimate whether it comes from the State or anyone else and rejects compulsory state provision",
      "Minimal Tolerance shares that rejection of moral legitimacy but departs from Rothbard by allowing exceptional tolerance of a coercive function when necessity is established",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "minarchism-defense-police-courts",
    number: "18",
    title: "Does minarchism justify a State limited to defense, police, and courts?",
    answer: [
      "Classical minarchism treats defense, police, and courts as the legitimate core functions of the State",
      "Minimal Tolerance rejects granting those functions privileged status by default. A smaller State is not morally exempt from the problem of coercion",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "nozick-minimal-state-legitimacy",
    number: "19",
    title: "Is Nozick's minimal State morally legitimate?",
    answer: [
      "In <em>Anarchy, State, and Utopia</em>, Nozick defends a minimal state as morally legitimate",
      "Minimal Tolerance rejects that conclusion: reducing the scope of the State does not change the moral status of its coercion",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "absence-voluntary-alternatives-state-necessary",
    number: "20",
    title: "Does the absence of voluntary alternatives prove the State is necessary?",
    answer: [
      "No. The fact that a function is not being provided voluntarily does not, by itself, give the State a right to provide it coercively",
      "A legal monopoly, state provision, or restrictive regulation may also have prevented alternatives from emerging",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "macroeconomic-instability-government-intervention",
    number: "21",
    title: "Does macroeconomic instability justify government intervention?",
    answer: [
      "Not by itself. Recession, mass unemployment, inflation, or financial disruption can make a problem severe without establishing that coercive government action is necessary",
      "Minimal Tolerance separates the empirical question of whether intervention can improve an outcome from the prior question of whether the function can be sustained without coercion",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "aggregate-demand-fiscal-stimulus",
    number: "22",
    title: "Does a collapse in aggregate demand justify fiscal stimulus?",
    answer: [
      "Keynesian economics provides a case for countercyclical fiscal policy when private demand is insufficient and idle resources persist",
      "Minimal Tolerance does not deny that fiscal stimulus can affect output or employment. It asks a different question: whether the coercive financing and authority required for that policy are necessary rather than merely useful",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "inequality-coercive-redistribution",
    number: "23",
    title: "Does inequality justify coercive redistribution?",
    answer: [
      "No. Inequality can be morally or politically important without establishing that redistribution must be compulsory",
      "A distributive objective and a demonstration of coercive necessity are different claims. The existence of unequal outcomes does not by itself show persistent collapse of voluntary provision",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "social-justice-state-coercion",
    number: "24",
    title: "Does social justice justify State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "Not by itself. A theory of social justice can specify how institutions or resources ought to be arranged, but that conclusion does not automatically authorize coercive means",
      "Minimal Tolerance requires the justification of coercion to be established separately from the desirability of the social end",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "social-insurance-compulsory-government",
    number: "25",
    title: "Does social insurance require compulsory government provision?",
    answer: [
      "Not necessarily. Adverse selection, moral hazard, incomplete coverage, and risk pooling can create serious problems for insurance markets",
      "Those problems can support a case for institutional intervention, but compulsory state provision still requires showing that materially different voluntary arrangements cannot sustain the relevant function",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "negative-externalities-state-coercion",
    number: "26",
    title: "Do negative externalities justify State coercion?",
    answer: [
      "Not automatically. Pollution, noise, physical interference, or other harms may involve violations of rights or responsibilities toward third parties",
      "When coercion is used to stop aggression, enforce liability, or define enforceable rights, the issue can fall outside the theorem's scope. Where no such violation is established, the existence of a negative externality alone does not prove a need for state provision",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "financial-instability-central-banking",
    number: "27",
    title: "Does financial instability justify central banking and a lender of last resort?",
    answer: [
      "Systemic risk, bank runs, and financial contagion can create unusually strong coordination problems and may place monetary or financial stability among the harder cases",
      "That still does not settle the institutional question. The relevant test is whether the stabilizing function is non-excludable, relevantly non-rival, and persistently unable to be sustained through materially different voluntary arrangements",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "industrial-policy-state-intervention",
    number: "28",
    title: "Can industrial policy justify State intervention?",
    answer: [
      "Strategic importance, infant-industry claims, scale economies, supply-chain dependence, or coordination failures can explain why governments support particular sectors",
      "They do not by themselves establish coercive necessity. A function does not become exempt from the burden of proof because it is economically strategic or politically important",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "poverty-coercive-redistribution",
    number: "29",
    title: "Does poverty justify coercive redistribution?",
    answer: [
      "Poverty can create urgent moral reasons to help, but urgency does not by itself establish a right to compel others to finance a particular response",
      "Minimal Tolerance therefore separates the moral importance of poverty relief from the institutional question of whether coercive provision is necessary",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "collective-action-political-authority",
    number: "30",
    title: "Does collective action require political authority?",
    answer: [
      "No. Collective-action problems can make coordination costly and can generate incentives to free ride, but they do not always prevent voluntary cooperation",
      "Political authority becomes relevant only if the function itself meets the required conditions and materially different voluntary arrangements collapse persistently for the same structural reason",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "paternalism-merit-goods-government-intervention",
    number: "31",
    title: "Does paternalism or the idea of merit goods justify government intervention?",
    answer: [
      "Merit-good theories hold that some goods may be consumed below a socially desired level, or that access to them should not depend entirely on individual willingness or ability to pay",
      "That can support subsidies, mandates, or public provision as policy proposals, but it does not by itself establish coercive necessity. Education, health, retirement saving, and similar goods remain subject to the same burden of proof as any other function",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "social-rights-compulsory-state-provision",
    number: "32",
    title: "Do rights to healthcare, education, housing, or income justify compulsory State provision?",
    answer: [
      "Calling a good a social right states a normative claim about what people should be guaranteed. It does not by itself identify who must provide it or establish that provision requires coercion",
      "Minimal Tolerance distinguishes the moral claim to access from the institutional claim that compulsory State provision is necessary",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "keynes-role-of-government",
    number: "33",
    title: "What does Keynes argue about the role of government in the economy?",
    answer: [
      "In <em>The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money</em>, John Maynard Keynes argues that an economy can settle below full employment and that public policy can support aggregate demand when private spending is insufficient",
      "Minimal Tolerance accepts that this is an empirical case for possible effectiveness, not a complete moral case for coercive authority. The necessity of the coercive function must still be established",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "rawls-justice-coercive-redistribution",
    number: "34",
    title: "Does Rawls's theory of justice justify coercive redistribution?",
    answer: [
      "In <em>A Theory of Justice</em>, Rawls defends principles for the basic structure of society, including institutions that protect equal basic liberties and regulate social and economic inequalities",
      "Minimal Tolerance asks a prior institutional question: even if a distributive principle is accepted, what establishes the necessity of coercive means for implementing it",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "musgrave-functions-of-government",
    number: "35",
    title: "What does Musgrave's theory of public finance say about the role of government?",
    answer: [
      "Richard Musgrave famously organizes public finance around allocation, distribution, and stabilization functions",
      "Minimal Tolerance subjects each function to a separate burden of proof. A recognized function of public finance is not, by itself, evidence that coercive state provision is necessary",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "pigou-welfare-economics-government-intervention",
    number: "36",
    title: "What does Pigou's welfare economics imply about government intervention?",
    answer: [
      "Pigouvian welfare economics identifies cases in which private costs and benefits diverge from social costs and benefits, creating a rationale for corrective taxes, subsidies, or regulation",
      "Minimal Tolerance separates that welfare-improvement claim from coercive authorization. Showing that a policy could correct an externality does not by itself show that the State must coercively provide or enforce the function",
    ],
  },
];

const stripHtml = (value) =>
  value.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, "");

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "@id": "https://minimaltolerance.com/questions/#faq",
  url: "https://minimaltolerance.com/questions/",
  name: "What Should Government Be Allowed to Do?",
  description:
    "Questions about government intervention, state coercion, public goods, market failure, Keynesian policy, redistribution, externalities, and voluntary provision",
  inLanguage: "en",
  isPartOf: {
    "@type": "WebSite",
    name: "Minimal Tolerance",
    url: "https://minimaltolerance.com/",
  },
  mainEntity: questions.map((question) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: question.title,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      "@type": "Answer",
      text: question.answer.map(stripHtml).join(" "),
    },
  })),
};
---

<!doctype html>

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />

    <meta
      name="viewport"
      content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"
    />

    <SEO
      title="What Should Government Be Allowed to Do? | Minimal Tolerance"
      description="When is government intervention justified? Explore state coercion, public goods, market failure, Keynes, redistribution, externalities, and voluntary provision."
      lang="en"
    />

    <script
      type="application/ld+json"
      set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)}
    ></script>
  </head>

  <body>
    <Header />

    <main>
      <!-- HERO -->

      <section class="hero questions-page-hero">
        <div class="hero-content hero-sequence">
          <p class="eyebrow">
            THE QUESTIONS
          </p>

          <h1>
            What should the State<br />
            be allowed to do?
          </h1>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- CENTRAL STATEMENT -->

      <section class="statement">
        <blockquote class="reveal reveal-scale">
          No one has an inherent right to rule another
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <!-- SEARCH / TOPIC INTRO -->

      <section class="questions-intro" aria-labelledby="questions-intro-title">
        <div class="questions-container">
          <h2 id="questions-intro-title" class="questions-intro-title reveal">
            Government intervention, State power, and the limits of coercion
          </h2>

          <p class="questions-intro-lead reveal">
            When is government intervention justified? This page examines the
            main cases for and against State power: democracy, public goods,
            free riders, market failure, externalities, macroeconomic
            stabilization, fiscal stimulus, redistribution, social insurance,
            merit goods, social rights, central banking, industrial policy,
            and voluntary provision
          </p>

          <p class="questions-intro-links reveal">
            Minimal Tolerance separates the value of an end from the
            justification of coercive means. Read the
            <a href="/theory">theory of State power and its limits</a>,
            examine the
            <a href="/theorem">three conditions for coercive necessity</a>,
            or see how the framework performs across
            <a href="/cases">concrete cases</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- KEY QUESTIONS -->

      <section class="questions-index">
        <div class="questions-container">
          <p class="eyebrow reveal key-questions-label">
            KEY QUESTIONS
          </p>

          <nav
            class="questions-index-grid stagger"
            aria-label="Questions about government intervention, state coercion, public goods, market failure, redistribution, Keynesian policy, and political power"
          >
            {
              questions.map((question) => (
                <a
                  href={`#${question.id}`}
                  class="question-index-card reveal"
                >
                  <span class="question-index-number">
                    {question.number}
                  </span>

                  <span class="question-index-title">
                    {question.title}
                  </span>
                </a>
              ))
            }
          </nav>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- QUESTIONS -->

      <section class="questions-content">
        <div class="questions-container">
          {
            questions.map((question) => (
              <article
                id={question.id}
                class="question-section"
              >
                <div class="question-heading reveal reveal-left">
                  <span class="question-number">
                    {question.number}
                  </span>

                  <h2>
                    {question.title}
                  </h2>
                </div>

                <div class="question-copy reveal reveal-right">
                  {question.answer.map((paragraph) => (
                    <p set:html={paragraph}></p>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- FINAL CTA -->

      <section class="questions-cta">
        <div class="questions-container questions-cta-content">
          <div class="reveal reveal-left">
            <p class="eyebrow">
              THE THEORY OF MINIMAL TOLERANCE
            </p>

            <h2>
              The burden of proof belongs to power
            </h2>

            <p>
              Explore the criterion that determines when a function can
              pass the test of coercive necessity without turning
              tolerance into legitimacy
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="questions-cta-buttons reveal reveal-right">
            <a href="/theory" class="primary">
              Explore the Theory
            </a>

            <a href="/theorem" class="secondary">
              Examine the Theorem
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <Footer />

    <style>
      /*
       * Force a true two-column grid here so each pair of questions
       * shares the exact same row height.
       */
      .questions-intro {
        padding: 0 0 3.5rem;
      }

      .questions-intro-title {
        font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 1.8vw, 1.75rem);
        line-height: 1.2;
        margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
      }

      .questions-intro-lead,
      .questions-intro-links {
        max-width: 62rem;
      }

      .questions-intro-lead {
        font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.15vw, 1.16rem);
        line-height: 1.72;
        margin: 0 0 1rem;
      }

      .questions-intro-links {
        line-height: 1.7;
        margin: 0;
      }

      .questions-intro a {
        text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
        text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
      }

      .key-questions-label {
        color: #a07d37 !important;
        font-size: 1.15rem;
        letter-spacing: 0.14em;
        margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
      }

      .questions-index-grid {
        display: grid !important;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)) !important;
        grid-auto-flow: row !important;
        gap: 0 !important;
        align-items: stretch !important;
      }

      .question-index-card {
        box-sizing: border-box !important;
        display: grid !important;
        grid-template-columns: 3.1rem minmax(0, 1fr) !important;
        align-items: start !important;
        align-content: start !important;
        column-gap: 1rem !important;
        width: 100% !important;
        height: 100% !important;
        min-height: 0 !important;
        margin: 0 !important;
        padding: 1rem 1.15rem 1rem 0 !important;
        transition:
          color 220ms ease,
          background 220ms ease,
          transform 220ms ease;
      }

      .question-index-card:nth-child(odd) {
        border-right: 1px solid var(--line, rgba(20, 30, 40, 0.14)) !important;
        padding-right: 1.4rem !important;
      }

      .question-index-card:nth-child(even) {
        padding-left: 2rem !important;
      }

      .question-index-number,
      .question-index-title {
        align-self: start !important;
        margin: 0 !important;
        padding: 0 !important;
      }

      .question-index-number {
        line-height: 1.28 !important;
        transition: transform 220ms ease;
      }

      .question-index-title {
        display: block !important;
        line-height: 1.28;
      }

      /*
       * Keep the index type exactly at the approved size.
       */
      .question-index-title {
        font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 1vw, 1.08rem);
      }

      /*
       * Developed-question title: 10% smaller than the previous version.
       * Right-side answer size is untouched.
       */
      .question-number {
        display: inline-block;
        transform: scale(1.50);
        transform-origin: left top;
      }

      .question-heading h2 {
        font-size: clamp(1.08rem, 1.62vw, 1.62rem);
        line-height: 1.12;
      }

      .question-section {
        overflow: hidden;
      }

      @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
        .question-index-card:hover {
          transform: translateY(-2px);
        }

        .question-index-card:hover .question-index-number {
          transform: translateX(5px);
        }
      }

      @media (max-width: 900px) {
        .questions-index-grid {
          grid-template-columns: 1fr !important;
        }

        .question-index-card,
        .question-index-card:nth-child(odd),
        .question-index-card:nth-child(even) {
          grid-template-columns: 2.75rem minmax(0, 1fr) !important;
          column-gap: 0.85rem !important;
          padding: 0.9rem 0 !important;
          border-right: 0 !important;
        }

        .question-index-title {
          font-size: 0.95rem;
        }

        .question-heading h2 {
          font-size: clamp(0.99rem, 3.33vw, 1.26rem);
        }

        .question-heading.reveal-left,
        .question-copy.reveal-right {
          transform: translateY(24px);
        }

        .question-heading.reveal-left.is-visible,
        .question-copy.reveal-right.is-visible {
          transform: translateY(0);
        }
      }

      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .question-index-card,
        .question-index-number {
          transition: none;
        }
      }
    </style>

    <script is:inline>
      const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia(
        "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
      ).matches;

      const revealElements =
        document.querySelectorAll(".reveal");

      if (reduceMotion || !("IntersectionObserver" in window)) {
        revealElements.forEach((element) => {
          element.classList.add("is-visible");
        });
      } else {
        const revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver(
          (entries, observer) => {
            entries.forEach((entry) => {
              if (!entry.isIntersecting) return;

              entry.target.classList.add("is-visible");
              observer.unobserve(entry.target);
            });
          },
          {
            threshold: 0.12,
            rootMargin: "0px 0px -40px 0px",
          }
        );

        revealElements.forEach((element) => {
          revealObserver.observe(element);
        });
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
